{{also|Sulfate|sulfaté}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|sulphate}} {{qualifier|UK}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fr|sulfate}}, from {{der|en|NL.|sulphatum}}, taken from the expression {{m|la|acidum sulphatum||sulfuric acid}}, from {{m|la|sulphatus}}, from {{der|en|la|sulphur||sulfur}}. The term was first used in 1787 by the French chemist [[:w:Louis-Bernard Guyton de Morveau|L. B. G. De Morveau]]. Equivalent of {{suffix|en|sulfur|ate}}.<ref>{{R:svenska.se|saob}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsʌlfeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sulfate.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}} {{lb|en|American spelling}}

# {{lb|en|organic chemistry}} Any [[ester]] of [[sulfuric acid]].
# {{lb|en|inorganic chemistry}} Any [[salt]] of [[sulfuric acid]].

